* sg13cmos5l-bandgap -- closed-loop startup bench body (issue #30, Part of #5).
* Template: sim/grid-run.sh substitutes @@VDD@@ (the ramp target) and @@WORK@@ and writes the
* deck to sim/build/closed-loop-startup/deck.cir. The model `.lib` cards and the temperature
* come from the `klt sim` request (request-grid.json).
*
* FIXTURE -- the rail itself ramps 0 -> @@VDD@@ over 200 us and holds to 2 ms, so what the
* simulator solves for is the assembled block's own cold-start -> closed-loop-settled
* transition. The 200 us ramp and the 2 ms window are carried over unchanged from the
* inherited source bench sim/sg13cmos5l-closed-loop-startup at the commit in
* reuse.lock.json (its testbench/sg13cmos5l_closed_loop_startup.body.spice.tmpl), so this
* bench's startup trajectory is the one the source recorded 45/45 points passing on.
*
* The source baked the ramp target per supply and dispatched one request per supply,
* because `alter` cannot sweep a PWL source. This bench keeps ONE request with the full
* supply axis instead: `corners.supply_waveforms: ["Vvdd"]` rescales the PWL levels per
* corner (peak -> the corner's supply), which klt reports per point under
* `corners[].supply_waveforms`. That is why the ramp target below is the nominal 3.30 V and
* not a placeholder: it is the PWL peak the rescaling is relative to.
*
* vss at 0 and the substrate net `sub!` tied to vss through a 0 V source, as in
* sim/closed-loop-vref-pvt/vref.cir.tmpl and in the source body.
*
* NOT carried over from the source body: its `Vmkfb` ammeter in series with XMKFB's drain.
* The source body inlined every DUT device, so it could split that net; this bench
* `.include`s the migrated hierarchical netlist, and inserting the ammeter would mean
* editing the DUT, which decision record 0006 forbids. The startup cell's release is
* instead graded on its own detect node through the hierarchical name `v(xdut.xx3.det)`,
* which is the gate drive of XMKFB and the source's own release criterion
* (`v(det) <= 0.2*vdd`); see README.md 'What the Vmkfb ammeter measured'. The `xdut.`
* level is the DUT wrapper below -- the same node, one level deeper (record 0013).
*
* OSDI objects load through the request's options.osdi_preload, not a .control block: a
* .control block here would land inside the deck `klt sim` `.include`s, not in the top deck
* it generates, so its commands would never run.
*
* NO SPICE TITLE LINE (issue #19): `klt sim` `.include`s this deck into a top deck of its
* own, so line 1 here is not in title position and a title line would be parsed as a
* netlist element.
*
* DUT INSTANTIATION -- `Xdut vdd vref vss sg13cmos5l_bandgap_top` below. The staged DUT is
* no longer flat: sim/runner-lib.sh's cm5_stage_dut makes the xschem export's commented-out
* top block live as `.subckt sg13cmos5l_bandgap_top vdd vref vss` and drops its `.end`
* card, without editing the attested source (decision record 0013). That cell name and pin
* order are the layout-extracted netlist's, so this one deck can be pointed at either leg
* for the T1 item-7 delta; a flat DUT makes `klt pex` fire `flat_dut_mismatch`, and a
* wrapper in the export's own `vdd vss vref` order makes it fire `pin_order_mismatch`.
* Electrically a no-op -- measured, not assumed (decision record 0013). Internal nodes are
* addressed one level down accordingly: v(xdut.fb), v(xdut.sns1), v(xdut.sns2),
* v(xdut.xx3.det).

* rshunt/gmin: convergence aids only, far below any current read here. Carried over from
* the source body, whose comment explains why they are needed on this bench in particular:
* the real, not-yet-loop-gain-tuned amplifier can fail to converge at an early,
* near-singular instant on the vdd ramp at a marginal corner.
.options rshunt=1e9 gmin=1e-9

Vvdd vdd 0 PWL(0 0 200u @@VDD@@ 2m @@VDD@@)
Vvss vss 0 dc 0
Vsub sub! vss dc 0

.include "@@WORK@@/bandgap_top.spice"
Xdut vdd vref vss sg13cmos5l_bandgap_top
